NAME
       podman-generate-systemd - write a systemd unit file for a container

SYNOPSIS
       podman generate systemd [--name] [--new] [--files] [--restart-policy POLICY]
                               CONTAINER

DESCRIPTION
       podman generate systemd prints a systemd service unit that starts and
       stops an existing container, so the container can start at boot like
       any service. Podman 5 marks the command as deprecated and prints a
       warning: Quadlet files (podman-systemd.unit(5)) are the newer way, but
       this one still works.

       For a rootless container, the unit is a user unit: it goes in
       ~/.config/systemd/user/ and is handled with systemctl --user. A user's
       units run only while the user is logged in, unless lingering is
       turned on with loginctl enable-linger USER; then they start at boot.

OPTIONS
       -n, --name
              Use the container's name, not its ID, in the unit (the file is
              then container-NAME.service).

       --new  The unit creates a fresh container at each start (podman run)
              and removes it at stop, instead of starting and stopping the
              existing one. The unit then works even after the container is
              removed.

       -f, --files
              Write the unit to a file in the current directory instead of
              printing it, and print the file's path.

       --restart-policy POLICY
              The unit's Restart= setting (default on-failure).

EXAMPLES
       As student (log in with ssh student@localhost, not su), run a
       container, turn it into a service that starts at boot, and test it:

           podman run -d --name web -p 8080:8080 registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/httpd-24
           mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user
           cd ~/.config/systemd/user
           podman generate systemd --name web --new --files
           podman stop web
           podman rm web
           systemctl --user daemon-reload
           systemctl --user enable --now container-web.service
           loginctl enable-linger student
           systemctl --user status container-web.service

SEE ALSO
       podman-systemd.unit(5), podman(1), systemctl(1), loginctl(1)
